## Creating git repo from local

1. git init
2. git add .
3. git commit -m "initial commit"
IF you want to call master as main
    git branch -m master main

4. Connect To github
    1. create repo in github and copy url 
        git remote add origin https://github.com/Harivara/StudentManagementSystem.git

                            OR

    2. Link local repo to github
        git remote add origin https://github.com/Harivara/StudentManagementSystem.git
5. Push to Github
    git push -u origin main


# Delete branch
git push origin --delete [BRANCHNAME]

# Unset the upstream branch
git branch --unset-upstream

# To create new Upstream
git push --set-upstream origin your-new-branch-name

# Alternatively, if the repository doesn't have a corresponding remote branch anymore and you're done with this remote, you can remove it from your configuration:

git remote remove origin

# Check remote origin
git remote -v

# To view more details about the remote configuration, you can use:
git remote show origin


# Delete branch in LOCAL
1. if merged
    git branch -d feature-branch
2. not merged(force delete)
    git branch -D feature-branch

# Delete branch in remote
1. git push origin --delete feature-branch

# When refusing to merge due to unrelated-histories
git pull origin main --allow-unrelated-histories


# How to revert a pull request   
1. commit history
    git log --oneline
2.  Revert the Merge Commit (Safe if already pushed)
    git revert -m 1 <merge-commit-hash>
    abc1234 is the hash of the merge commit.

-m 1 tells Git to keep the first parent of the merge (usually your local work)
 and discard the changes from the second parent (the pulled remote).

This will create a new commit that undoes the merge, and is safe to push.


# To ignore files to be pushed into git Repo
1. Create a .gitignore
2. add path
    Ex: I dont want to include bin directory 
    Add /bin in .gitignore
3. If git is already tracking bin folder use
    git rm -r --cached bin/


